   109	## A9.1 V1's success definition, claim, profile and scenario mapping — OWNER DECISION (Q1), implementing A and C
   110	
   111	**Success definition** (`docs/V1-SCHEDULE.md`, adopted here). V1 succeeds when:
   112	1. An organisation can obtain a receipt, at a stated cost and latency, asserting that *these exact bytes were
   113	   presented, signed by key K whose authority is evidenced by named channels, no later than time T*.
   114	2. A relying party can verify that claim without Tessera running and without the authors' help. From V1's
   115	   documentation alone, it reaches the safe decision on every case of the registered mapping below, including the
   116	   cases whose answer is "not established": **zero unsafe accepts; false rejects only on d2 and h** (AUTHOR
   117	   DECISION C; this replaces V1-SCHEDULE's "the prototype's scenario set"). *Evidence:* one human tester (n = 1,
   118	   recorded under the pseudonym "Aki Doe") who meets the independence criteria of `docs/V1-TESTER-BRIEF.md`: a CS
   119	   background; no knowledge of attestation, timestamping, blockchains or this project; has not discussed Tessera
   120	   with the author; and no help from the project during the test. The evidence is stated as one tester's result,
   121	   not as a result about relying parties in general.
   122	3. V1 ships by 4 December 2026, or the project stops pursuing V1 by explicit decision at that point.
   123	
   124	Milestones (ceilings, accepted by the author 2026-10-09: *"I'll accept those dates"*): 16 Oct, 30 Oct, 20 Nov,
   125	4 Dec, with V1-SCHEDULE's slip rule.
   126	
   127	**Claim.** *Conditional on the registered cryptographic and key-use assumptions, a V1 receipt lets anyone holding it
   128	establish, without Tessera, that the attested content was presented to the Tessera V1 service, whose own domain is
   129	D; that each of the service's two keys K = {K_kms, K_gpg} signed its own frame binding that content; that two named
   130	channels authenticated these keys for D, with a pre-committed issuance window, at the stated evidence times; that
   131	the signed frames and their exact signatures existed no later than T; and that the declared issue time is not
   132	earlier than the window's not-before and the verified chain-time T is not later than its not-after. Effective
   133	revocation and historical algorithm and key safety are not evaluated, and the receipt attests neither the
   134	submitter nor the content's meaning. The verdict is typed and says what was not established.*
   135	
   136	| # | Sub-claim | Non-claim kept beside it |
   137	|---|---|---|
   138	| C1 Bytes | The verifier reconstructs each signer's exact frame; both bind the same content and common metadata, the designated fingerprint in its slot. The receipt-anchor preimage `A_sig` over both frames and both signatures is unambiguous. | Nothing about meaning or truth. Payload identity is not frame equality. |
   139	| C2 Presentation | The content was presented at some issuance event of the V1 service (existential). | Not who submitted it; not whether a replay suits the transaction. |
   140	| C3 Signature | Each required signature verifies over its designated frame; a missing, stripped or substituted one fails. | **The two keys are separate technical failure domains under a single controller; V1 claims no separation of control and no independent fates**, and no independent approval of content. The `gpg` signer signs frames delivered by the AWS-side issuer, so capture of the issuing account confers signing capability for both slots; the non-exportable key prevents key export, not misuse. |
   141	| C4 Authority | DNSSEC (owner name D, evaluated at the snapshot's own anchored time) and the anchored git key assertion (full commit→tree→blob path) authenticated K for D, with the issuance window, at their evidence times. The verifier fixes the required set. | Revocation not evaluated. Not public publication of the commit. Neither channel commits to any receipt (CODEX-REVIEW 3). |
   142	| C5 Time | The frames **and their exact signatures** existed no later than T, the earliest verified attestation in the presented OTS proof; the three-conjunct predicate holds at k = 6. | Presentation-sensitive T. No lower bound, no priority. Header times are chain-time proxies. Reorganization deeper than k is a residual. |
   143	| C6 Without Tessera | The verdict is a pure function of the bundle, the published verifier and its distributed trust configuration. | Custody is the holder's. Dead-project reconstruction not demonstrated. Anchors beyond the newest pin are `UNVERIFIABLE`. |
   144	| C7 Safe decision | The verdict is typed and reports `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`, `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED` and standing separately; a non-expert makes no unsafe accept on the mapping (4 Dec criterion, not yet established). Assigned build evidence includes A9.4's missing-capture negative control. | Revocation, historical security and standing are not claimed. |
