   283	## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
   284	
   285	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   286	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   287	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   288	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   289	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   290	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   291	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   292	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   293	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   294	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   295	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   296	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   297	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual. The git evidence names its object format, and V1
   298	  accepts only `sha1`: the key-assertion repository stays in SHA-1 format at a host that supports it, whatever
   299	  git's default becomes (the author, 2026-10-13: Git 3 moves to SHA-256 in 2027; GitHub does not support it).
   300	  The pre-freeze bundle schema (V1-PROFILE §2.1, G-26) defines the git-evidence member `object_format` as the string
   301	  `"sha1"`. It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives
   302	  `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING`; an unsupported value gives `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT`. The
   303	  verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override
   304	  their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit
   305	  id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct
   306	  profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
   307	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
   308	  period contains that time validates it**. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance
   309	  lifetime L, with the reliance interval [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is the registered acceptance-policy
   310	  value derived from the cited root DNSKEY RRSIG validity evidence; a largest observed lifetime is labelled observed,
   311	  not asserted to be a proven global maximum. A candidate must also satisfy cryptographic validation and the selected
   312	  RRSIG's inception/expiration checks at the snapshot's own verified anchored time. This extension permits authentic
   313	  pre-retirement evidence anchored later; it does not establish that signing occurred before retirement. A thief of
   314	  a retired KSK can manufacture eligible root evidence during the extension. Exposure is bounded by the reliance
   315	  endpoint in snapshot chain time, not by a finite UTC duration under A2.1. Historical authority-key safety and the
   316	  continuing anchor/history assumptions remain necessary and are not evaluated by V1. **AUTHOR ADOPTED, 2026-10-13**
   317	  (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2; kind CORRECT: the withdrawn cutoff claimed a protection DNSSEC itself does not
   318	  supply, since resolvers accept a retired KSK's signatures until they expire). The author: *"I'd argue that the
   319	  'narrowing of protection' wasn't a real narrowing, it was us admitting the real level of protection given the
   320	  constraints of the project."* The rule is existential and order-independent: a candidate that fails is
   321	  informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible key has been tried (F1). A trusted KSK that
   322	  is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure. The trust configuration records signing and
   323	  reliance periods separately from publication and revocation dates.
   324	- **Trust configuration is append-only** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13). Trust configuration entries are append-only: KSKs, header
   325	  pins and repository keys are never removed, and finalized period endpoints never change. Repository trust entries
   326	  name D, the key and an inclusive signing interval; the git assertion passes this trust check if some configured
   327	  entry for D verifies gpgsig and its interval contains the signed commit's own verified anchored time. Repository
   328	  entries have no KSK-style reliance extension. Unknown material cannot establish this required check. Append-only
   329	  extensions preserve previously usable trust witnesses; newly covered evidence can improve an earlier UNVERIFIABLE
   330	  result. Exact stable-field identity remains the contract for identical declared inputs. Closing an open period must
   331	  not retrospectively exclude evidence previously accepted under that entry; the pre-freeze configuration records the
   332	  endpoint/update convention explicitly. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   333	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   334	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   335	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
   336	
