    87	## 2. Bundle format
    88	
    89	### 2.1 Members
    90	
    91	A V1 bundle carries these twelve members. Nothing is taken from outside the bundle except the trust configuration
    92	(section 5).
    93	
    94	| # | Member | Source |
    95	|---|---|---|
    96	| 1 | Unsigned profile label (read first, for dispatch) | [ENC §8:742-743] |
    97	| 2 | The receipt tuple `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)`, or `A_sig`, which encodes it | [ENC §5.3:493-497] |
    98	| 3 | The manifest frame `F_manifest` and each key's possession signature over it | [ENC §4:362-369; A9:311-312] |
    99	| 4 | The two public keys, checked against the manifest fingerprints | [ENC §5.3:509] |
   100	| 5 | The DNSSEC snapshot (envelope per ENC §6) and its own OTS proof | [ENC §6:536-542; DD:94] |
   101	| 6 | The git key-assertion evidence: raw signed commit object, the tree objects on the path, the blob, and the OTS proof stamped on the commit id | [ENC §7.2:706-710; A9:282-284] |
   102	| 7 | The receipt's OTS proof and the anchor transaction id | [SPEC R-3.42:679-682] |
   103	| 8 | Bitcoin block headers needed by the proof, each of which must be in the pinned set | [A9:150; DD:99] |
   104	| 9 | The verification specification bytes (content-addressed) | [A9:152-153; ENC §3.2:281] |
   105	| 10 | The conformance vector bytes (content-addressed) | [A9:152-153; ENC §3.2:281] |
   106	| 11 | The plain-language purpose preamble, bound by hash in the signed bytes | [A9:153; ENC §3.2:281] |
   107	| 12 | The dependency manifest (section 9) | [A9:153-154] |
   108	
   109	- Members 9, 10 and 11 are bound by SHA-256 of their raw bytes, inside the signed common bytes. They are never signed
   110	  or anchored on their own. [ENC §0:71-72; ENC §3.2:281]
   111	- The OTS proof, txid, headers and confirmation evidence are outside `A_sig`. [ENC §5:402-405]
   112	- The txid sits in the bundle outside the signed bytes. [SPEC R-3.43:685-687]
   113	- `OPEN (other):` the container and transport encoding, member names and member order. ENC leaves them out of scope
   114	  (ENC:802). A9 does not decide them. The profile freeze needs them.
   115	- `OPEN (other):` where the observed anchor delay and the issue-time policy version are recorded
   116	  (ENC:803; SPEC R-3.46:698-700).
   117	
   118	### 2.2 What the bundle never supplies
   119	
   120	The bundle never supplies the trust configuration, δ, ε, k or any verifier policy. [A9:148; SPEC R-5.9:1219]
   121	
   122	## 3. Verifier dispatch, stages and precedence
   123	
   124	### 3.1 Dispatch
   125	
   126	1. The verifier reads the bundle's unsigned profile label first. An unrecognized label gives
   127	   `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE`, and nothing else is evaluated. [ENC §8:742-743; A9:272]
   128	2. Out-of-profile layer structure or request gives the same result without evaluation. [DD:70; A9:272]
   129	   Cases c (two-layer transplant) and h (renewal wrapper) land here. [A9:173, 179]
   130	3. `OPEN (step 8):` what exactly dispatch inspects. ENC names only the unsigned profile label (ENC:742). DD names
   131	   "profile, layer structure or request" (DD:70). Case d1 (degraded request, then strict) needs a request field
   132	   (A9:174). Decide the fields dispatch reads, and where the request type lives.
   133	4. Once dispatched to `tessera-v1-strict`, every codec rejection is `INVALID`. That includes a signed `profile` other
   134	   than `tessera-v1-strict`, an unknown tag and an unknown canonicalization version. [ENC §8:744-746]
   135	5. The one in-profile `UNVERIFIABLE` that concerns the bytes is a well-formed unknown `alg`. [ENC §8:747-748;
   136	   SPEC R-2.37:308]
   137	
   138	### 3.2 Stages
   139	
   140	The stage names follow the spec's. The spec registers no order between stages, only that the verdict does not
   141	depend on evaluation order. [SPEC:1237-1248; SPEC R-5.16:1250] There are ten stages after dispatch. The
   142	table presents ENC/SPEC material; it is not a newly fixed execution order (section 0.1, item 6).
   143	
   144	| Stage | What V1 does | Source |
   145	|---|---|---|
   146	| (a) Parse | Decode every frame, the manifest and `A_sig` under ENC §1 to §5. Reason codes are reported in ENC's order. | [ENC §1.4:172-190; ENC §3.1:241-248; ENC §5:385-394] |
   147	| (b) Type | Tag must be in T and be the expected one: `TAG_UNKNOWN`, `TAG_MISMATCH`. No wrapper stage: one layer only. | [ENC §2:215-218; A9:155] |
   148	| (c) Algorithm | A well-formed unknown `common.alg` gives `UNVERIFIABLE`. A malformed, missing or substituted id gives `INVALID`. | [ENC §3.2:276; SPEC V13-V15:1282-1284] |
   149	| (d) Key binding | Each presented key hashes to its slot's manifest fingerprint (`KEY_MISMATCH`). `manifest_sha256` equals the hash of the manifest frame. | [ENC §5.3:509; ENC §3.2:280] |
   150	| (e) Authority | DNS channel and git channel (section 6), the issuance window, possession proofs. | [A9:137; A9:338-340] |
   151	| (f) Signature set | Both slots are required, from the signed manifest, never from what the bundle presents. Common bytes are byte-equal across slots. | [SPEC V28-V31:1327-1330; ENC §3.4:303-320] |
   152	| (g) Integrity | `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. Each committed `S` verifies over its committed `F`. Any difference from the committed tuple gives `RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`; a bad signature gives `SIG_INVALID`. | [ENC §5.3:498-511] |
   153	| (h) Temporal | The three-conjunct predicate at k = 6, plus the T rule (section 7). | [A9:138; SPEC V33-V36:1342-1346; SPEC R-5.19:1352] |
   154	| (i) Revocation | Not evaluated. The report says so. | [A9:248-252] |
   155	| (j) Partition | Section 3.3. | [SPEC R-5.25:1368] |
   156	
   157	- The issuance-window check is part of stage (e) (C4). [A9:338-340; ENC §7.1:636-643]
   158	- `OPEN (step 8):` which reason code is reported when several different checks fail. ENC fixes the order inside the
   159	  codec (ENC §1.4) and says verification-stage codes come after codec codes (ENC:508). It does not order the
   160	  authority, window, anchor and temporal codes against each other. The verdict is order-free (SPEC:1250); the reported
   161	  reason code is not yet fixed.
   162	- `OPEN (step 8):` the verdict class of the `DNS_*` record rejections (ENC §7:594-612). Present evidence that fails
   163	  validation is `INVALID`; missing evidence is `UNVERIFIABLE` (SPEC R-2.31:292; case d2, A9:175). ENC does not say
   164	  which class a malformed record is. State it.
   165	
   166	### 3.3 Precedence and verdicts
   167	
   168	- Order of decision: any required check failed gives `INVALID`; else any required check unperformable gives
   169	  `UNVERIFIABLE`; else `VALID_STRICT`. There is no waiver set, so the degraded branch never applies. [SPEC
   170	  R-5.25:1368; A9:155]
   171	- `INVALID` dominates `UNVERIFIABLE`. [A9:176; SPEC R-5.26:1370; SPEC R-2.33:300] Case d3 depends on it.
   172	- The unperformable rule applies to required checks only. [SPEC R-2.32:298]
   173	- A verdict is never a bare boolean. `UNVERIFIABLE` is never promoted. [SPEC R-2.34:302; SPEC R-5.38:1394]
   174	- No fallback, negotiation or normalization during verification. [SPEC R-5.18:1266; ENC §1.2:156-158]
   175	
   176	### 3.4 Output
   177	
   178	Every report carries these six fields, and they are the stable set that later verifiers must reproduce: the verdict,
   179	its reason code, T, `revocation`, `historical_security` and standing. [A9:156; A9:262-268]
   180	
   181	- `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED`. [A9:248-252]
   182	- `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply: an authenticated
   183	  safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared with T and A2.1's chain-time uncertainty. [A9:253-259]
   184	- Standing: `ABSENT/NOT_ISSUED_IN_PROFILE`, reported separately and never folded in. [A9:166-167; SPEC R-1.16:163]
   185	- No aggregate checkmark. [SPEC R-5.47:1419]
   186	- Relying-party text must say that `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` can come from presenting a later proof, and that the
   187	  holder should be asked for the issuer's original proof. [A9:344-345]
   188	- Relying-party text must say that the verifier cannot check public publication of the git commit. [A9:413-418]
   189	
   190	## 4. Issue order (issuer side, binding on the build)
   191	
   192	1. Fix the manifest and common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   193	2. Build both frames.
   194	3. Sign each frame.
   195	4. Verify both signatures with the exact bytes to be committed.
   196	5. Freeze the signature bytes. Any re-framing happens before this point.
   197	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp its SHA-256. One stamp per receipt.
   198	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   199	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   200	
   201	[ENC §5.4:515-525; A9:278-281] That is eight steps in both sources. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a
   202	fresh stamp. The frame-only anchor is withdrawn, with no fallback. [A9:277; ENC §5.1:407-414]
   203	
   204	Issuer duties that are not verifier rules: an attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped [A9:341-342]; the
   205	`gpg` signer refuses a frame whose `declared_issue_time` is more than ε from its clock [A9:327-330]; the issuer never
   206	re-authorizes a key [A9:260]; public publication of the commit stays an issuer duty [A9:413-418]; the issuer
   207	re-captures the DNSSEC snapshot before shipping if it fails the snapshot rule [DD:94].
   208	
   209	## 5. Trust configuration
   210	
   211	It ships with the verifier and is never read from the bundle. [A9:148; SPEC R-5.8:1217; SPEC R-2.17:262] It holds:
   212	
   213	| # | Item | Source |
   214	|---|---|---|
   215	| 1 | Root KSK-2017 | [A9:148-149; DD:66] |
   216	| 2 | Root KSK-2024 | [A9:148-149; DD:66] |
   217	| 3 | The pair (D, repository root key), with D = `wamason.com` | OWNER PROFILE DECISION 2026-10-13 (section 6.3); [A9:63-65] |
   218	| 4 | A signed, versioned set of pinned mainnet header hashes | [A9:149; DD:66] |
   219	| 5 | The parameters δ, ε, k | [A9:217; SPEC R-5.8:1217] |
   220	
   221	- **Root KSKs, three periods each.** The trust configuration records each KSK's signing and reliance periods
   222	  separately from its publication and revocation dates. [A9:153; A9:302-303] The instrument spike found that
   223	  conflating signing with publication falsely rejected every snapshot from 2026-10-11. [SR:73-78]
   224	- **Root rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time if some trusted KSK whose **reliance
   225	  period** contains that time validates it. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance lifetime L; the reliance
   226	  interval is [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is an acceptance-policy value from cited RRSIG validity evidence,
   227	  labelled observed, not a proven global maximum. The extension admits authentic pre-retirement evidence anchored
   228	  later and does not prove signing before retirement: a thief of a retired KSK can manufacture eligible evidence
   229	  during it (A9.5; AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-13, kind CORRECT). *Dated correction 2026-10-13: the earlier wording
   230	  ("maximum validity … it made"; "usable for that long and no longer") overclaimed provenance and is withdrawn.* The rule is existential and
   231	  order-independent: a candidate that fails is informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible
   232	  key has been tried. A trusted KSK that is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure.
   233	  [A9.5, A9:296-303, OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13 after `docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F1, F2]
   234	- **Rollover control (required vector).** A snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must
   235	  pass. [A9:307-308] Further required vectors (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F1, F2 dispositions): a failing trusted
   236	  candidate listed before a validating one, in both trust-list orders, must pass; an honest old root signed before
   237	  retirement and anchored after retirement but within reliance must pass, and the same evidence after reliance must
   238	  not.
   239	- **Known facts for the periods.** KSK-2024 signs the root from 2026-10-11. KSK-2017 is revoked 2027-01-11 and
   240	  removed 2027-03-22. [DD:66; SR:70-71] **OWNER PROFILE DECISION 2026-10-13** (values under the A9.5 rule):
   241	  - KSK-2017 signing period ends **2026-10-11T00:00:00Z**: IANA, the root zone begins using KSK-2024 on 2026-10-11
   242	    and KSK-2017 ceases signing (SR:70-71; IANA sources as cited in `docs/reviews/v1-profile/CODEX-REVIEW.md` F2).
   243	    Corroborated by the 2026-10-12 capture, whose only root DNSKEY RRSIG is by KSK-2024 with inception
   244	    2026-10-11T00:00:00Z.
   245	  - Observed root DNSKEY RRSIG validity, used as L: **21 days** (observed, not a proven maximum), from the archived captures. `prototype/spike/out/dns-snapshot.json`
   246	    (captured 2026-10-09): the KSK-2017 RRSIG runs 2026-10-01T00:00:00Z to 2026-10-22T00:00:00Z (21 d), the only
   247	    KSK-2017 root RRSIG archived. `prototype/spike/out-ksk2024/dns-snapshot.json` (captured 2026-10-12): the KSK-2024
   248	    RRSIG runs 2026-10-11T00:00:00Z to 2026-11-01T00:00:00Z (21 d), corroborating. One KSK-2017 signature is the
   249	    whole observed sample.
   250	  - KSK-2017 reliance period ends **2026-11-01T00:00:00Z** (= 2026-10-11T00:00:00Z + 21 d). KSK-2024's reliance end
   251	    stays open until its signing end is recorded.
   252	  - The earlier end-of-day cutoff (2026-10-11T23:59:59Z) is **withdrawn**: it rejected authentic pre-retirement
   253	    evidence anchored later (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2). Extending reliance also extends a retired-key
   254	    compromise window; A9.4 leaves historical key safety unevaluated either way.
   255	  - Implemented in `prototype/spike/trust-config.json` (`signing_source`, `max_validity_source`, `reliance_rule`).
   256	- **No usable trusted KSK — OWNER PROFILE DECISION 2026-10-13** (under R-2.26; repaired after
   257	  `docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F1, F3). Each candidate signature is classified separately, and only after every trusted
   258	  KSK has been tried:
   259	  - a signature that verifies under a trusted KSK, is within its own RRSIG window, and whose KSK's reliance period
   260	    contains the snapshot's anchored time: the set is valid, whatever the other candidates show;
   261	  - otherwise, a signature that **verifies** but whose KSK's reliance period excludes that time:
   262	    `INVALID / KSK_OUTSIDE_RELIANCE_PERIOD`;
   263	  - otherwise, a signature that verifies but is outside its own inception/expiration window at that time: `INVALID`
   264	    (`RRSIG_NOT_VALID_AT_EVAL_TIME` in the instrument);
   265	  - otherwise, a signature bearing a trusted KSK's tag that does not verify: `INVALID`
   266	    (`TRUSTED_KSK_SIGNATURE_INVALID` in the instrument). A key tag is a selector, not proof of authorship, so a
   267	    forged tag never produces the reliance reason;
   268	  - otherwise (signed only by keys the trust configuration does not hold, or the RRset, RRSIG or message missing):
   269	    `UNVERIFIABLE / NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE`.
   270	  - A snapshot without its own anchor is `UNVERIFIABLE / SNAPSHOT_NOT_ANCHORED`; no stand-in time is used. Missing
   271	    snapshot evidence of any kind is `UNVERIFIABLE`, never an exception, unless another required check fails.
   272	- **Unlisted rollover.** The periods are those known at release. A snapshot taken after an unlisted rollover is
   273	  `UNVERIFIABLE`. [A9:150-151]
   274	- **Header pins.** A bundle header outside the pins gives `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`. An anchor beyond the
   275	  newest pin is `UNVERIFIABLE`. [A9:150; A9:139]
   276	- `OPEN (other):` the key that signs the pin set, its format and its versioning rule. A9 says only "signed,
   277	  versioned" (A9:149).
   278	- `OPEN (other):` whether the repository key being the public half of an offline root is profile policy. Today it
   279	  is an operating fact from the runbook (RB §1, reference only), not an A9 requirement (section 0.1, item 5).
   280	
   281	## 6. Authority channels
   282	
   283	Both channels must pass (n = 2). Neither commits to any receipt. [A9:137, 147] Both carry the same issuance
   284	window. [A9:332-333; ENC §7.1:630-634]
   285	
   286	### 6.1 DNS channel
   287	
   288	- Owner name is `_tessera-v1.<D>`. The verifier rebuilds it from the signed `issuer` and compares label by label,
   289	  ignoring ASCII case. Anything else is `DNS_OWNER`. [ENC §7:551-556] This is the owner-name check (G-21 / M2).
   290	- One TXT RRset, one RR, one character-string. The value grammar, the fifteen-step rejection order and the

exec
/bin/bash -lc "nl -ba docs/V1-PROFILE.md | sed -n '291,560p'" in /home/tony/projects/tessera
 succeeded in 0ms:
   291	  exact-255-byte rule are in ENC §7. [ENC §7:557-612]
   292	- The record carries `kms`, `gpg`, `profile`, `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after`, all mandatory, in a
   293	  fixed order. [ENC §7:569-579]
   294	- The DNSSEC chain is validated against the root rule (section 5) at the snapshot's own anchored time (section 7.3).
   295	  [A9:137; A9:285-287]
   296	
   297	### 6.2 Issuance window (C4 verifier rule)
   298	
   299	- The window `issuance-not-after − issuance-not-before` is at most 90 days. A record that violates it is rejected
   300	  (`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`). V1 has one window and no overlap. [A9:334-337; ENC §7:578-580]
   301	- A receipt is `INVALID / ISSUANCE_WINDOW` unless `issuance-not-before` ≤ declared issue time and T ≤
   302	  `issuance-not-after`. Both bounds are inclusive. T is compared as registered, with no margin. T is not compared
   303	  with not-before. [A9:338-340; ENC §7.1:636-643, 662-663]
   304	- The guarantee is in chain time. A UTC cutoff would need a backward-skew bound V1 does not establish. [A9:360-366;
   305	  ENC §7.1:661-671]
   306	- A published window cannot be shortened. [A9:343]
   307	- The window, its dates and the stop margin are operating facts, not profile rules. [A9:341-342; RB §2]
   308	
   309	### 6.3 Git channel
   310	
   311	The OTS stamp targets the signed commit's object id, never a tree, blob or unsigned commit. [A9:282-284; ENC
   312	§7.2:685-691] The verifier does four things:
   313	
   314	1. Recomputes the commit id from the presented raw commit object.
   315	2. Verifies `gpgsig` over the object without that header, under the pinned repository key.
