   358	
   359	## 4. The manifest frame (map-v1 tuple; G-08, G-14, G-22)
   360	
   361	```
   362	F_manifest = frame("tessera-v1/manifest", JCS({"alg":A,"issuer":D,"key_fp":fp_kms,"sset":[fp_gpg],"ver":"map-v1"}))
   363	manifest_sha256 = hex(SHA256(F_manifest))
   364	```
   365	
   366	- The schema is closed: `key_fp` must be a `spki-sha256:` string, and `sset` must hold exactly one `gpg-sha256:`
   367	  string. `ver` is exactly `map-v1`. Anything else is `SCHEMA`.
   368	- *Reason: this is the map-v1 tuple of R-3.36 in the slot order of the C-C2 trace (G-09). Each key's possession
   369	  proof (R-3.38) is its OpenPGP signature over this same frame. The `tessera-v1/manifest` tag keeps that
   370	  signature apart from the same key's attestation signature (P7).*
   371	
   372	## 5. The receipt-anchor preimage `A_sig` (G-19 and G-59 resolved; K-2; revised 2026-10-12)
   373	
   374	```
   375	A_sig = header("tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig") ‖ u8 0x02
   376	        ‖ u32 len(F_kms) ‖ F_kms ‖ u32 len(S_kms) ‖ S_kms
   377	        ‖ u32 len(F_gpg) ‖ F_gpg ‖ u32 len(S_gpg) ‖ S_gpg
   378	anchor digest = SHA256(A_sig), the value OpenTimestamps stamps
   379	```
   380	
   381	- `S_x` is slot x's detached signature over `F_x`, exactly as §5.2 serializes it.
   382	- Slots appear in **manifest slot order**: `kms` is the manifest's `key_fp`, and `gpg` is `sset[0]`.
   383	- The count byte is the number of slots. It must be 2 (`ANCHOR_COUNT`).
   384	
   385	**Decoding, slot by slot.** Each step's boundary is fixed by bytes already read.
   386	1. `u32` frame length. Above 2^24 + 64 gives `LENGTH_CAP`. Then exactly that many bytes, decoded as one complete
   387	   attestation frame (§3.1). That decode yields the frame's `common.alg`.
   388	2. `u32` signature length. 0 gives `SIG_EMPTY`. Above the cap of the rule for `(common.alg, slot)` gives
   389	   `LENGTH_CAP`.
   390	3. Exactly that many bytes, decoded by that rule (§5.2).
   391	4. After the second signature, any byte gives `TRAILING`. A slot running past the end of input gives
   392	   `TRUNCATED`.
   393	
   394	The rule for a slot is chosen from that slot's own frame and its position, both already read.
   395	
   396	*Reason.* One OTS proof must cover a receipt that has two signed frames (R-3.42 with reading (a)). It must commit
   397	to the signatures, not only the frames: a stamp over unsigned frames proves only that the frames existed. It
   398	leaves open signing them later, once signing capability for every required slot has been obtained (CODEX-REVIEW
   399	finding 1; finding 2 for "every slot"). Length prefixes and whole, exactly consumed parts make the preimage
   400	injective without any hash assumption (PROOF Lemma 7). The tag keeps it from being taken for a snapshot's proof.
   401	
   402	**Outside the preimage.**
   403	- The outputs of anchoring: the OTS proof, txid, block headers and confirmation evidence. Including them would
   404	  be circular (finding 4).
   405	- Transport decoration: armor, containers and the bundle format.
   406	
   407	### 5.1 Why the tag changes (and the count byte does not)
   408	
   409	The 2026-10-09 frame-only layout used the tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor`. That tag is **withdrawn and removed
   410	from T**. A frame-only preimage is therefore rejected at its header with `TAG_UNKNOWN` (XA-09). A fully signed
   411	receipt presented with a frame-only anchor is rejected the same way (XR-09).
   412	
   413	There is **no fallback**. No verifier accepts a frame-only anchor under any profile label.
   414	
   415	Reasons:
   416	- **Separation by construction.** With a distinct tag, rejecting the old layout is PROOF Lemma 4: the header
   417	  alone decides it. It does not depend on whether bytes in the old layout happen to fail a signature rule.
   418	  - Under the old tag, that dependence would be real. Under rule R, any byte string of the right length is a
   419	    syntactically valid signature.
   420	  - XA-10 shows the same-tag case: the old layout is rejected today only because the gpg frame's first byte
   421	    fails rule O's packet header.
   422	- **Readable dumps.** A hex dump names which construction it holds (C6).
   423	- **No migration cost.** Nothing was issued under the draft layout, so replacing it costs nothing.
   424	
   425	The count byte keeps its meaning (two slots), so it is not the place to carry the version.
   426	
   427	### 5.2 Signature-slot rules (by algorithm identifier)
   428	
   429	Each slot's `S` is **exactly one detached signature** in the single serialization its rule pins.
   430	- `S` is **nonempty** and **at most the rule's cap**.
   431	- The decoder **consumes `S` exactly**. Bytes beyond the signature's own extent give `TRAILING`, which covers a
   432	  second packet. Fewer bytes give `TRUNCATED`.
   433	- **Never `S`:** ASCII armor, a display string, a keyring, or any container that can hold more than one
   434	  signature.
   435	
   436	The rule is chosen by `(common.alg, slot)`:
   437	
   438	| `common.alg` | slot `kms` | slot `gpg` | Cap per `S` |
   439	|---|---|---|---|
   440	| `OPENPGP4-ECDSA-P256-SHA256` (the classical V1 signers) | rule O | rule O | 1024 bytes |
   441	| a hybrid pair id, registered only if the 21 Oct spike passes (not assigned here) | rule R, ML-DSA-65 (FIPS 204) | rule O (OpenPGP P-256; slot-2 custody per A9.6; owner decision 2026-10-12 replaced SLH-DSA) | `kms`: exactly 3309; `gpg`: 1024 |
   442	| any other well-formed id (§3.2) | opaque | opaque | 1 to 65536 bytes; no inner rule. Stage (c) gives `UNVERIFIABLE` (canary, A-02) |
   443	
   444	**Rule O (OpenPGP v4 signature, ECDSA P-256, SHA-256; RFC 9580 §4.2, §5.2.3).**
   445	
   446	*The packet header (`SIG_PACKET`).*
   447	- `S` is exactly one Signature packet (tag 2) with a **legacy-format header in its shortest form**:
   448	  - `0x88 ‖ u8 n` when the body has n ≤ 255 bytes;
   449	  - `0x89 ‖ u16 n` when 256 ≤ n ≤ 1021.
   450	- Each of these gives `SIG_PACKET`:
   451	  - any other first byte: an OpenPGP-format `0xC2` header, another packet tag, or ASCII armor's `-`;
   452	  - a longer length form than needed.
   453	- This also excludes partial and indeterminate lengths.
   454	
   455	*The packet body (`SIG_FORMAT`).* The body is, in order:
   456	1. version `04`, type `00`, public-key algorithm `13` (19, ECDSA), hash algorithm `08` (SHA-256);
   457	2. `u16` hashed length, then the hashed subpackets;
   458	3. `u16` unhashed length, then the unhashed subpackets;
   459	4. two bytes: the left 16 bits of the digest;
   460	5. MPI r;
   461	6. MPI s.
   462	
   463	Each of the following holds, or the result is `SIG_FORMAT`:
   464	- each subpacket area is an exact sequence of subpackets, each at least 1 byte, with shortest-form lengths;
   465	- each MPI is 1 to 256 bits, with an exact bit count;
   466	- the body ends exactly after s.
   467	
   468	Type `0x01` (text) is `SIG_FORMAT` (D-10). The codec does not interpret subpacket contents.
   469	
   470	*Reasons.*
   471	- **The legacy header is what the gpg signer emits.** GnuPG 2.4.4 emits exactly this header for a detached
   472	  P-256 signature: `88 75 04 00 13 08 …`, a 119-byte packet, observed 2026-10-12. Its output is therefore `S`
   473	  unchanged.
   474	  - The KMS slot's packet is assembled by Tessera's own code, and it must emit the same form.
   475	  - A tool that emits an OpenPGP-format header must have its output re-framed **before stamping**. The header is
   476	    not covered by the signature hash, so re-framing does not affect validity.
   477	  - A verifier never re-frames.
   478	- **The cap.** 1024 bytes is about 8× a GnuPG signature, and it still bounds the malleable unhashed area.
   479	
   480	**Rule R (raw FIPS 204 / FIPS 205 signature).**
   481	- `S` is the raw signature byte string that the standard's signing algorithm outputs (the FIPS 204 `sigEncode`
   482	  output; the FIPS 205 signature). It has no header and no wrapper.
   483	- Its length is **exactly** the parameter set's signature size. Any other nonzero length gives `SIG_FORMAT`.
   484	- The signed message is the frame itself. The spike used pure signing with an empty context. The algorithm id's
   485	  registration fixes the mode, the context and the parameter set.
   486	- This revision fixes no PQ id and no parameter set.
   487	- *Consequence to record:* `common.alg` is one value, equal across slots (§0). A PQ registration must therefore
   488	  name the **pair** of slot schemes (ML-DSA-65 for `kms` with OpenPGP P-256 for `gpg`) as a single id.
   489	- Rule R has no vectors yet. They are added when an id is registered.
   490	
   491	### 5.3 Verification uses the committed bytes, and only those
   492	
   493	The verifier first obtains the ordered tuple `(F_kms, S_kms, F_gpg, S_gpg)`, by either route below. The two are
   494	the same by PROOF Lemma 7.
   495	- decode `A_sig`; or
   496	- encode the presented tuple into `A_sig`.
   497	
   498	It checks that `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. It then verifies **each committed `S` over
   499	its committed `F` under its slot's key**, using exactly those bytes.
   500	
   501	The verifier never does any of these:
   502	- verify a separately selected, re-serialized, normalized or reconstructed signature;
   503	- accept a receipt whose presented frames or signatures differ in any byte from the committed tuple
   504	  (`RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`), even when the presented signature is itself valid (negative controls XR-10 to
   505	  XR-14);
   506	- accept a frame-only anchor.
   507	
   508	The vectors exercise the following verification-stage codes. They are reported after every codec code:
   509	- `KEY_MISMATCH`: the presented public key does not hash to its slot's manifest fingerprint;
   510	- `SIG_INVALID`: a committed signature does not verify (XR-16, signatures swapped between slots, anchor rebuilt);
   511	- `UNVERIFIABLE`: the algorithm id is not registered.
   512	
   513	These codes belong to the signature verification profile (P3), not to P8.
   514	
   515	### 5.4 Issue order (finding 6)
   516	
   517	1. Fix the manifest and the common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   518	2. Build `F_kms` and `F_gpg`.
   519	3. Produce one detached signature per slot over its frame.
   520	4. Verify both signatures over their frames under the slot keys, using the exact bytes to be committed.
   521	5. **Freeze** `S_kms` and `S_gpg`. Any serialization step, such as rule O re-framing, happens before this
   522	   point. Nothing changes a signature byte after it.
   523	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp `SHA256(A_sig)` with OpenTimestamps: **one** stamp per receipt (R-3.42).
   524	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   525	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   526	
   527	- A signature never includes its own eventual anchor time or proof.
   528	- A retry that changes any frame or signature byte needs a fresh stamp, and it obeys the existing reissue and
   529	  temporal rules.
   530	- There is no per-signer stamp, and no frame-only proof is kept alongside.
   531	- R-3.9 is clarified, not weakened. Signatures commit to frames, and the receipt anchor commits to frames plus
   532	  signatures.
   533	
   534	## 6. The DNSSEC-snapshot preimage
   535	
   536	```
   537	S = header("tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot") ‖ u32 len(snapshot) ‖ snapshot        1 ≤ len ≤ 2^24
   538	```
   539	
   540	- The snapshot body's internal format belongs to the profile document (Track 3). P8 fixes only its envelope.
   541	- *Reason:* C4's snapshot rule gives the snapshot its own OTS proof. The tag keeps that proof from being
   542	  presented as a receipt anchor, or the reverse.
   543	
   544	---
   545	
   546	## 7. DNS key-publication record
   547	
   548	C4(i) needs a DNSSEC-authenticated record that binds V1's two keys to D. Neither the spec nor wamason.com has one
   549	yet (spike report; R-4.26 says only "DNSSEC records publishing the fingerprints").
   550	
   551	**Owner name:** `_tessera-v1.<D>`, where D is `common.issuer`.
   552	- The verifier rebuilds this name from the signed `issuer` and compares it, label by label and ignoring ASCII
   553	  case, with the validated RRset's owner name. Anything else gives `DNS_OWNER`.
   554	- *Reason:* the owner name itself is then the C4 owner-name check (G-21/M2). An underscore label cannot collide
   555	  with a hostname, and the `-v1` suffix lets a later profile publish next to it.
   556	
   557	**Type:** TXT. **RRset:** exactly **one** TXT record at the owner name (`DNS_RRSET` if there are zero or more).
   558	**RDATA:** exactly **one** character-string (`DNS_STRINGS` otherwise). If a string's length byte runs past the
   559	RDATA, the result is `DNS_RDATA`.
   560	- *Reason:* one string removes every question about how several strings concatenate. A provider that splits a

exec
/bin/bash -lc "nl -ba formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md | sed -n '561,810p'" in /home/tony/projects/tessera
 succeeded in 0ms:
   561	  value is non-conforming for V1.
   562	- Every canonical value is **exactly 255 bytes**, the limit for one string (DNS-02): each field has a fixed length
   563	  and, since 2026-10-12, every field is mandatory. That leaves no room for another field. A later field needs a new
   564	  record version.
   565	
   566	**Value grammar** (ABNF; the canonical form is the only accepted form):
   567	
   568	```
   569	value      = "v=tessera1" ";profile=tessera-v1-strict" ";kms=" 64LHEX ";gpg=" 64LHEX
   570	             ";issuance-not-before=" utc ";issuance-not-after=" utc
   571	LHEX       = %x30-39 / %x61-66                              ; lowercase hex only
   572	utc        = 4DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "-" 2DIGIT "T" 2DIGIT ":" 2DIGIT ":" 2DIGIT "Z"
   573	             ; RFC 3339 UTC; a real Gregorian date and time, seconds 00-59, no fraction
   574	```
   575	
   576	- `kms` is the hex of §3.3's `spki-sha256`. `gpg` is the hex of §3.3's `gpg-sha256`.
   577	- `issuance-not-before` and `issuance-not-after` are both **mandatory** (since 2026-10-12; `issuance-not-before` was
   578	  optional before). `not-after` must be strictly later than `not-before` (`DNS_WINDOW`), and
   579	  `not-after − not-before` must be at most 90 days, 7 776 000 s (`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`; Amendment 9's single V1
   580	  window). §7.1 gives their meaning.
   581	- `utc` is exactly the grammar of `declared_issue_time` (§3.2), so the window and the declared time compare
   582	  without conversion. The record's previous grammar was a date only. A one-second boundary cannot be stated with
   583	  a date, so the window uses the same time grammar as the signed bytes.
   584	- The 2026-10-09 optional `not-before=<date>` is replaced by `issuance-not-before` and is now `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`
   585	  (XD-29).
   586	- The value has no spaces and no trailing `;`. Field order is fixed. Bytes are printable ASCII from 0x21 to 0x7E.
   587	
   588	Example (DNS-02):
   589	
   590	```
   591	v=tessera1;profile=tessera-v1-strict;kms=5cd252fb…7cd3;gpg=5ecd7121…8fc3;issuance-not-before=2026-10-12T00:00:00Z;issuance-not-after=2027-01-10T00:00:00Z
   592	```
   593	
   594	**Rejection order** (the first that applies):
   595	1. `DNS_OWNER`
   596	2. `DNS_RRSET`
   597	3. `DNS_RDATA`
   598	4. `DNS_STRINGS`
   599	5. `DNS_SYNTAX`:
   600	   - a byte outside 0x21–0x7E;
   601	   - a field that is not `key=value`, with key `[a-z][a-z-]*` and a nonempty value containing no `=`;
   602	   - an empty field.
   603	6. `DNS_VERSION`: the first field is not `v=tessera1`, or `v` appears again.
   604	7. `DNS_FIELD_UNKNOWN`: an extra field.
   605	8. `DNS_SLOT_DUPLICATE` (`kms` or `gpg` appears twice) or `DNS_FIELD_DUPLICATE` (another field appears twice).
   606	9. `DNS_SLOT_MISSING`.
   607	10. `DNS_FIELD_MISSING` (`profile`, `issuance-not-before` or `issuance-not-after`).
   608	11. `DNS_ORDER`.
   609	12. `DNS_PROFILE`.
   610	13. `DNS_VALUE`: the hex or a time fails its grammar.
   611	14. `DNS_WINDOW`: `issuance-not-after` ≤ `issuance-not-before`.
   612	15. `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`: `issuance-not-after − issuance-not-before` > 90 days (7 776 000 s).
   613	
   614	**Why the record is unambiguous** (outside PROOF.md's frame domain):
   615	- The accepted values form a regular language. Every field begins with a fixed literal key, and every value
   616	  comes from an alphabet without `;` or `=`, so splitting on `;` and then on the first `=` recovers the fields
   617	  uniquely.
   618	- The field order is fixed, and every value has exactly one spelling: lowercase hex, and `YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ`.
   619	  So the map from (kms, gpg, issuance-not-before, issuance-not-after) to value strings is injective, and its
   620	  inverse is the parse. `:` is outside the separators, and `DNS_WINDOW` and `DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH` are checks on parsed
   621	  values, so none of them affects the split.
   622	- One RR with one character-string removes concatenation ambiguity. The RDATA length byte delimits the string.
   623	- The owner name is compared as a label sequence, so `_tessera-v1.wamason.com` cannot match
   624	  `_tessera-v1.wamason.com.evil.example` or `tessera-v1.wamason.com` (XD-12 to XD-14).
   625	- The value begins `v=` (0x76), while every tagged string in §2 begins with a byte from 0x11 to 0x1d. So a TXT
   626	  value can never be a V1 frame, and a frame can never be a TXT value.
   627	
   628	### 7.1 Pre-committed issuance window (owner decision 2026-10-12; single 90-day window per Amendment 9)
   629	
   630	The key-publication record carries the issuance window. For V1 there is one window, of at most 90 days
   631	(`DNS_WINDOW_LENGTH`), with no overlapping successor: before it ends, either a rotation mechanism is registered or
   632	issuance stops (Amendment 9, A9.6). It is the same canonical value in both channels: the DNS
   633	TXT record, and the git key-assertion blob (§7.2). The window is therefore fixed in DNSSEC-authenticated and
   634	anchored authority evidence before any receipt that relies on it.
   635	
   636	**Verifier rule (C4).** A receipt passes C4 only if both of these hold:
   637	- `issuance-not-before` ≤ `declared_issue_time`;
   638	- **T ≤ `issuance-not-after`**, where T is the time of the earliest verified attestation in the presented OTS
   639	  proof.
   640	
   641	Otherwise the verdict is `INVALID`, with reason `ISSUANCE_WINDOW`. Both bounds are inclusive: W-01 is
   642	T = not-after, W-02 is declared time = not-before. XW-01 is T = not-after + 1 s, and XW-02 is a declared time
   643	1 s early.
   644	
   645	*Rationale, recorded.*
   646	- **A surviving or stolen key cannot mint a receipt that passes with T after the window.** Keys may outlive the
   647	  service's exit, or be stolen. Even signing capability for every required slot cannot produce a receipt that
   648	  passes with verified T after `issuance-not-after`, under continuing anchor, history and authority assumptions. A fresh mint's `A_sig` (§5) can only
   649	  be stamped at or after the moment it exists, and the anchor prevents claiming an earlier T. So every receipt
   650	  whose verified T is after the window fails (XW-03). This is a statement in chain time, not in UTC (see the
   651	  residual below).
   652	  - A compromise *before* `not-after` is not covered. Within the window, a thief mints receipts that pass, subject
   653	    to the historical-security caveats of CODEX-REVIEW findings 7 and 8.
   654	- **Key-format expiries are not relied on.**
   655	  - OpenPGP key-expiration subpackets live in a self-signature. Any keyholder, a thief included, can re-issue that
   656	    self-signature to extend them.
   657	  - A raw SPKI key, such as a KMS ML-DSA key, carries no expiry at all.
   658	  - The window is instead fixed in authority evidence that the keyholder alone cannot rewrite after the fact.
   659	    Rewriting it means re-publishing the record, which is a new authority event with its own evidence times.
   660	
   661	*Residual: chain time (A2.1).*
   662	- T is the **header-time-derived T as registered**. The verifier compares it with `issuance-not-after` directly,
   663	  with no margin added or subtracted. That keeps one registered meaning of T.
   664	- A Bitcoin header's timestamp is not an exact UTC clock. Consensus constrains it only loosely: it must exceed the
   665	  median of the previous eleven blocks, and nodes accept it up to about two hours ahead of their own clock.
   666	- In the direction that matters here, a stamp actually made **after** `not-after` can carry a header time at or
   667	  before `not-after`. Its receipt passes. A2.1 registers no finite bound on that backward skew, and neither ε nor S
   668	  is one. So what this rule guarantees is "no receipt passes with verified T after `not-after`". Translating that
   669	  into a finite UTC cutoff needs an additional, explicit backward-skew assumption that V1 does not establish
   670	  (Codex review of Amendment 9, finding 2; an earlier draft of this paragraph claimed such a bound).
   671	- The opposite error, an honest stamp made just before `not-after` getting a later header time and failing, is
   672	  the safe direction.
   673	- *Issuer invariant:* an attempt whose T would exceed `not-after` is not shipped. The stop margin that implements
   674	  it is an operating number, not part of this encoding (`docs/V1-OPERATIONS-RUNBOOK.md`). The verdict reports the
   675	  comparison with T as registered.
   676	
   677	### 7.2 Git channel: what its OTS stamp must cover (finding 9; added 2026-10-12)
   678	
   679	- **The blob's content.** Since the issuance-window decision (§7.1), the git key-assertion blob carries the same
   680	  canonical value as the TXT record, so both channels assert one byte string, window included.
   681	- **What P8 does not fix.** The blob's path, and any byte framing around the value: for example, whether a
   682	  trailing LF is allowed. Those are not P8's.
   683	- **What P8 fixes here.** The blob's anchor target.
   684	
   685	- **The stamp targets the signed commit's object id.** That commit adds the assertion blob, and it carries a
   686	  `gpgsig` header that verifies under the repository key.
   687	- These stamp targets are **not acceptable**:
   688	  - the tree;
   689	  - the blob;
   690	  - an unsigned commit, including the unsigned payload that the commit signature covers.
   691	- *Why, from git's documented object format:*
   692	  - gitformat-signature(5) (git 2.43.0, "COMMIT SIGNATURES") gives a commit signature's payload as the commit
   693	    object, embedded as the header entry `gpgsig`.
   694	  - gitglossary(7) says an object "is uniquely identified by the SHA-1 of its contents".
   695	  - So a signed commit's id is computed over the object **including** its `gpgsig` header. The signature lies
   696	    inside the anchored bytes.
   697	  - A stamp over the tree or blob covers no signature. A stamp over the unsigned commit is the receipt's own
   698	    pre-stage-then-sign gap again.
   699	- *Checked 2026-10-12* (git 2.43.0, GnuPG 2.4.4, scratch repository):
   700	  - the signed commit's id equals `SHA-1("commit " ‖ decimal size ‖ 0x00 ‖ raw object)`;
   701	  - the raw object contains the `gpgsig` header;
   702	  - `git hash-object -t commit` reproduces the id;
   703	  - the unsigned commit with the same tree had a different id, while its tree id was identical.
   704	- **Matching the repository's convention.** This repository stamps a file `timestamps/<id>` holding the commit's
   705	  lowercase hex id and LF, after the commit exists. For a signed commit, that is this target.
   706	- **What the verifier does.** It:
   707	  1. recomputes the id from the presented raw commit object;
   708	  2. verifies `gpgsig` over the object without that header;
   709	  3. follows the commit to its tree and then to the blob;
   710	  4. checks that the stamped bytes name that id.
   711	- The git signature is ASCII-armored inside the commit header. §5.2's binary rule applies to receipt slots only.
   712	- **Residual, already recorded.** Two parts are SHA-1:
   713	  - the commit id itself;
   714	  - the commit → tree → blob linkage.
   715	
   716	  Both are in the dependency manifest (V1-DERIVATION's bundle contents and custody residual) and outside P8's
   717	  SHA-256 scope (PROOF (H)). Anchoring a signed commit puts its signature inside the anchored bytes. It does not
   718	  turn the SHA-1 links into SHA-256 bindings.
