+++ b/docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/GAPS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,152 @@
+# V1 spec-sufficiency probe — 2026-10-13
+
+The four documents do **not yet determine an interoperable offline V1 verifier**.
+The first end-to-end stopping point is the explicitly OPEN bundle container; the
+next independent stop is the explicitly OPEN DNSSEC snapshot body. Beyond those
+formats, required binding forms, temporal witness selection and confirmation
+rules, and the git assertion's relation to the receipt still permit materially
+different acceptance policies. The byte codec is substantially more specified
+than the evidence/verdict layer.
+
+This is a bounded independent-implementer experiment, not a C-row discharge,
+registered conformance run, gate ruling, or finding that an existing verifier
+accepts an attack. **No actual receipt was shown to receive divergent verdicts.**
+Rows marked yes identify a plausible verdict fork under unresolved rules; the
+acceptance-sensitive rows give concrete distinguishing input conditions.
+
+## Read boundary and authority
+
+Only these repository sources were read for protocol requirements:
+
+- `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md` (A9), 523 lines; governs this probe as instructed.
+- `docs/V1-PROFILE.md` (P), 560 lines.
+- `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` (E), 810 lines.
+- `formal/p8/PROOF.md` (R), 531 lines.
+
+File:line references below use these exact paths and the line numbering at probe
+time. References inside those files to other documents, implementations or
+vectors were **not followed**. No contents under `prototype/` or `formal/suite/`
+were read. A git status listing exposed path names only; it did not supply
+implementation knowledge. Existing unrelated workspace changes were left alone.
+General agent-workflow instructions and a brief memory-registry lookup were read
+before the protocol pass; no remembered protocol rule was used as evidence or
+to resolve a gap. This is independent of existing implementation code, not a
+claim of complete prior-knowledge isolation.
+
+A9 labels itself PROPOSED and P labels itself DRAFT. The experiment treats A9 as
+governing because the task explicitly says so; it does not infer adoption from
+those labels or inspect signing history. Where A9 resolves stale P/E/R text, that
+resolution is recorded below rather than counted as an unresolved contradiction.
+
+## Design and stopping result
+
+The throwaway [probe](indep-verifier/verifier.py) takes **raw `A_sig` and raw
+`F_manifest` files**, not an invented bundle format. It implements framing,
+number-free canonical payloads, schemas, structural signature checks and receipt
+consistency. It also exposes the snapshot envelope and isolated issuance-window
+comparison. See [README](indep-verifier/README.md) for implemented checks and limits.
+
+No execution returns `VALID_STRICT`. All outputs have `v1_verdict: null`; a
+structurally passing receipt stops before signatures, authority and time. No
+untrusted input time, fingerprint, pin or claimed validation result substitutes
+for verified evidence. The local `probe_status` and `probe_reason` fields are
+deliberately not the frozen V1 report schema.
+
+The table records every design-level choice or stop encountered, including
+unimplemented stages reached by design inspection. It does not enumerate routine
+Python choices that cannot change protocol acceptance. **VERDICT-AFFECTING yes**
+means a different reasonable reading/completion can change VALID_STRICT / INVALID
+/ UNVERIFIABLE or prevent reaching a verdict; a reason-code-only difference is no.
+“Could accept” means after all other checks pass, not that this probe accepts it.
+
+## Gaps, ranked by verdict consequence
+
+### Acceptance-sensitive forks (could change VALID_STRICT to rejection)
+
+| ID / verifier stage | Question | Spec text relied on (file:line) | Why insufficient | Choice / stop; distinguishing condition | VERDICT-AFFECTING |
+|---|---|---|---|---|---|
+| G01 / required authority forms | What exact forms must V1 require, must signed `binding_forms` equal them, and how do DNS/git satisfy them? | `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:258`, `:277`; `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:354-358`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:141` | **Depends on OPEN.** Syntax is fixed; required semantic set and channel mapping are not. `STMT_DIRECT` is explicitly illustrative. | Stop before form acceptance. A receipt with well-formed `["STMT_OTHER"]` and both authority channels could pass a channels-only interpretation and fail an exact-form-set interpretation. Probe fixtures use STMT_DIRECT only for syntax, never as an adopted policy. | **yes** |
+| G02 / git authority time | Must the git assertion predate the receipt, its declaration, or its T? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:345-347`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:630-634`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:326-328` | **Depends on OPEN / ambiguous.** E says authority evidence is fixed “before any receipt”; P expressly leaves the relation undecided. A9 fixes the repository-key interval check at the commit's own time, not its relation to receipt T. | Stop. A signed assertion anchored after receipt T but during the repository key's signing interval could pass an evidence-time-only reading and fail a before-T reading. Do not infer public publication from git evidence. | **yes** |
+| G03 / temporal predicate | Is T the same as `anchor_time`, and which header supplies each? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:373-383`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:638-639`, `:662-663`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:142` | **Depends on OPEN.** Earliest verified attestation is named, but anchor_time is not equated with it. No complete header-time derivation is given. | Stop. With two attestations straddling declared + δ, using the earliest for the window but a later one for the temporal predicate can reject a receipt that a unified-T implementation accepts. | **yes** |
+| G04 / temporal confirmation | What is `confirmed_at`, how is it derived, and is k=6 inclusion plus five successors? Which selected attestation's confirmations count? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:376-383`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:103-105`, `:221`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:403-404` | **Silent / depends on OPEN.** DepthK=5 is supplied, but confirmation evidence and its time derivation are not encoded or fully defined. | Stop; no guessed confirmed_at. An anchor inside δ whose fifth successor is inside δ and sixth successor outside it separates counting interpretations. Inclusion-header time versus confirming-header time also separates acceptance. | **yes** |
+| G05 / attestation selection | Does earliest “verified” mean earliest header time or smallest height? Do malformed, unknown or insufficient-depth branches fail the proof or merely not qualify? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:373-383`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:142`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:638-639` | **Ambiguous / depends on OPEN.** No qualification/filtering or tie rule for an OTS attestation set. | Stop. A proof containing an early valid branch inside not-after plus an invalid or uncovered branch can pass a qualifying-witness interpretation and fail an all-presented-evidence interpretation. Nonmonotone header times can separate height from time selection. | **yes** |
+| G06 / snapshot time | How does the snapshot's own OTS proof select its anchored time, and at what depth? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:396-412`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:536-542`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:307-312` | **Depends on OPEN.** The own-anchor requirement is fixed; its derivation and confirmation depth are not. | Stop. A multi-attestation snapshot with one time inside and another outside an RRSIG window, or with fewer than six confirmations, separates choices. Never use capture time, receipt T or current wall time as a substitute. | **yes** |
+| G07 / pins and history coverage | Which heights must be supplied and pinned: inclusion only, five successors, each proof header, or intervening history? | `docs/V1-PROFILE.md:387-392`; `docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md:152-155`; `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md:403-404` | **Depends on OPEN.** Every bundle header must be pinned, but the mandatory set of bundle headers is not fixed. | Stop. A bundle containing only a pinned inclusion header is accepted by a pin-is-sufficient reading and cannot establish depth under a successor-header reading. Do not assume pins themselves establish confirmation timing. | **yes** |
